    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i was reading about hiit workouts and kept seeing stuff about lactic acid. can u explain how it actually gets made in the muscles and how the body gets rid of it after a set? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lactic acid, a byproduct of intense exercise, particularly HIIT (High-Intensity Interval Training) workouts, is a common concern for many. It's a natural process, but one that can be quite...gobsmacking, if you will, for those unfamiliar with it. You see, when you engage in HIIT, your muscles undergo a rapid shift from aerobic to anaerobic metabolism, resulting in the production of lactic acid as a byproduct of anaerobic glycolysis. It's a bit like a group of mischievous little goblins, working tirelessly to break down glucose and produce energy, but in the process, they leave behind a trail of lactic acid.\n\nNow, let's get to the nitty-gritty. Lactic acid is produced when your muscles convert glucose into energy through anaerobic glycolysis. This process occurs when your muscles are not receiving enough oxygen, causing them to rely on alternative energy sources. The goblins, I mean, the enzymes, catalyze this reaction, producing lactic acid as a waste product. This lactic acid builds up in your muscles, causing fatigue, soreness, and a general feeling of...well, let's just say, \"",
